around 251 million years ago  the sun rose on the triassic period  although often overlooked the triassic  marked the evolution of some of the most  unique organisms in our planet's history  especially under the waves within the  waters of the triassic dwelt reptiles  with hammer-shaped faces  fish with buzz-saw-like teeth which  confounded scientists for centuries  and some of the largest and most often  forgotten  marine predators of all time so for this  entry into the archive  we'll be winding back the clock and  looking at the undersea organisms of the  triassic  the triassic period began with a global  cataclysm an extinction event known as  the great dying  caused by elevated temperatures and  oceanic acidification  resulted in over 90 percent of all life  in the oceans  including the long enduring trilobites  dying out  but in the wake of this great reset new  species emerged  growing to fill the abundance of  unoccupied niches  resulting in some truly surreal  adaptations  this is henodus a placodont from the  late triassic that  likely dwelled within the shallows  henotis like most members of its order  resembles a sea turtle  but isn't actually closely related  honotus was only around 1 meter or  3.3 feet in length and that distinctive  unwieldy shell on their back  was almost as wide as the creature was  long exactly why henotis  or any animal would develop such an  unusual shell  isn't fully understood but some have  theorized that the henotis's shell was  such an awkward size  because it made it difficult for aquatic  predators to fit their jaws around it  a very straightforward strategy but a  pretty smart one  aside from henotis the placodon order  during the triassic period also included  cyamodus  this genus also looks quite similar to  sea turtles  and might actually be more closely  related to modern turtles than henotis  was  unlike most species of turtles today  however which have just the single shell  for protection  siamotis had two separate shells one  shell protected its neck to its waist  while the other one protected its lower  hip region  the dual shells were likely an effective  defense against predators  and give sayamotis a one-of-a-kind look  moving away from the placodont order  also living along the triassic coastline  was the noteworthy mastodosaurus a  triangular headed amphibian  mastodon source had two distinctive  front teeth which likely helped it  capture prey  strangely however mastodonosaurus's  teeth were so long  that in addition to the animal's  nostrils there were two  extra holes in their skull which these  teeth fit  through when their mouths were closed  the mastodonasaurus's peculiar upward  facing  eyes also sets them apart from many  species  the unexpected ocular placement would  have been most suited for looking  upwards  suggesting the animal often fed on  organisms which dwelled on the land  and with the largest mastodonosauruses  reaching around  6 meters or 20 feet there's quite a lot  that would likely be on the menu for  this species  further out into the ocean we have an  animal which  for centuries baffled scientists meet  the helicoprion an animal with one of  the most unusual dental records in the  history of life on earth  the fish possessed a strange spiral of  teeth with most genuses possessing more  than a hundred teeth in total  when fossils of this tooth spiral were  first uncovered in the 19th century  paleontologists weren't quite sure where  on the animal  they belonged with some hypothesizing  the toothy spiral sprouted from the  animal's upper snout  like a really deadly nose by the mid  20th century it became more widely  accepted that the fanged swirl belonged  on the bottom jaw of the animal the  exact purpose of the spiral was still  unknown however with some speculating  that the toothy whirl could lash out  like a tentacle  to grab prey today most scientific  reconstructions place the spiral of  teeth further back in the animal's jaws  or perhaps even in its throat the most  prominent modern theory is that the  tooth whirl has something to do with  replicating old  teeth kind of like the system sharks  have and no despite appearances the  helicoprion is not a genus of shark but  instead thought to fall within the now  primarily extinct classification known  as the ratfish  a somewhat unfortunate name even more  unfortunate  helicoprion was already on its way out  when the triassic kicked off  we think a few species within the  helicoprion genus made it all the way to  the early triassic period but  sadly none of these buzz saw-faced fish  made it any further  and while we're talking about species  with unusual lips the atapu dentatus is  near the top  of that particular category older  reconstructions of this extinct genus of  middle triassic marine  animal are quite the site to behold at  first scientists believe  that unlike almost all other animals  atapidentatus's jaw was split down the  middle  with teeth running along the length of  this split as alien  and alarming as this might seem most  scientists thought atapu dentatus  used its unusual mouth for filter  feeding  using the toothed split to filter out  unwanted dirt  instead of to maim helpless creatures  new more complete specimens however have  demonstrated atapu dentate's split mouth  was actually a misinterpretation  and the head actually looked more like  this  the unusual wide mouth was possibly an  adaptation to better scrape algae  off the sea floor for consumption  although we can't say for certain  while this new atapu dentatus model is  less frightening than the old one  it's definitely just as unique looking  before we get to the true  giants of the triassic sea our next  group might be a bit more familiar  looking  these are the sauropterygia a  classification that first appeared at  the very start of the triassic  orders like the nothosaur navigated the  waters on paddle-like flippers  snatching fish in their fang jaw with  lunges from their long  neck the nostasaur lived a bit like the  seals of today  and were relatively small only reaching  3 meters or  10 feet in length by contrast the sword  to rygia classification eventually  yielded the much larger plesiliusora  a group which is much more commonly  known and the only branch of sorptarigia  to make it out of the triassic  most of the biggest and most famous  plesiliusora like  the leopleurodon live during the  jurassic period however  and maxed out at 6.4 meters or 21 feet  in length  quite the impressive reptile but even  jurassic aeroplesleosaurus seems  small in comparison to the true titans  of the triassic waters  meet the ichthyosaurus a diverse group  of reptiles with a long  and impressive paleontological history  ichthyosaur fossils were first  discovered  as far back as the 19th century which  led to some intriguing vintage artistic  interpretations of what these colossal  reptiles might have looked like  in particular marianning found various  nearly complete skeletons during her  lifetime  if you're not familiar with marianning  she was a pioneering english  paleontologist who made vital  contributions to the field though she  never got the credit she deserved  one skeleton inning found was that of a  temenodontosaurus an ichthyosaur genus  from  the early triassic in both their overall  body shape  and their long toothed beaks these  unique animals resemble the dolphins  while the two animals occupy a similar  niche they  aren't related and are just an  interesting example of two  different animals converging on a  similar body plan due to environmental  factors  unlike the dolphin however the  tomenodontosaurus got  much bigger ranging between 9 to 12  meters in size or  feet easily the largest organism on our  list so far  but the very largest of the ichthyosaurs  were in another class  altogether meet the shonosaurus a late  triassic genus  coming in somewhere between 15 or 20  meters  or 49 to 69 feet  what an incredible creature like the  smaller temenodontosaurus the  shonosaurus was also  likely a marine predator who hunted for  fish cethlopods and other forms of  smaller marine life after all there's  not much that was bigger than an  organism this size  indeed across the fossil record the  shonosaurus rivals many of the largest  modern whales with their sheer  bulk another way in which schonosaurus  was similar to whales  is that their ancestors and indeed the  ancestors of all ichthyosaurs  once lived on land before transitioning  to fully aquatic animals  carnitor heinz is a potential distant  relative of the ichthyosaur just  beginning the lanta ocean transition  process  while kartour heinz likely spent most of  their time in the water  it's been theorized that they could  still use their flipper like four limbs  to move on land  sort of like juvenile sea turtles unlike  many of the groups on this list  various species of ichthyosaur survived  beyond the triassic period  yet despite being the group's  evolutionary debut the triassic period  was the epoch in which the largest  ichthyosaurs reigned  this once again goes to show you that  the triassic while often  overlooked was an incredible era in  earth's history  and that's the end of our deep dive into  the oceans of the triassic period  this video was made as part of the  triassic themed week edge is currently  running so  go check out his channel if you haven't  already in the meantime  thanks for watching if you like this  entry please lend your support  and like subscribe and hit the  notification icon to stay up to date on  all things curious  see you in the next video  [Music]  you
 

